To start an OpenSSH server on NixOS, enable the `openssh` service in your system configuration:

```nix
{
  services.openssh.enable = true;
}
```

Add the above line (or merge it into your existing `configuration.nix`). After rebuilding (`sudo nixos-rebuild switch`), the SSH daemon will be started and listening for connections.  

*By default, password‑based root logins are disabled; you can adjust this with `services.openssh.settings.PermitRootLogin` if needed.*  

Source: [1]​

To forbid any root login via SSH, set the OpenSSH option **PermitRootLogin** to `"no"` in your NixOS configuration:

```nix
{
  services.openssh.enable = true;
  services.openssh.settings.PermitRootLogin = "no";
}
```

This disables root logins completely. [1]

You can declare a user’s authorized SSH public keys in the NixOS configuration by adding them under the user’s `openssh.authorizedKeys` attribute. For example:

```nix
{
  # Enable the SSH daemon
  services.openssh.enable = true;

  # Declare the user and his authorized key(s)
  users.users.alice.openssh.authorizedKeys.keys = [
    "ssh-ed25519 AAAAB3NzaC1kc3MAAACBAPIkGWVEt4..."
  ];
}
```

This snippet declaratively sets the public key that `alice` may use for SSH logins [1].

To assign a fixed IPv4 address to **eth0** you configure the interface manually in `configuration.nix`:

```nix
{
  networking.interfaces.eth0.ipv4.addresses = [
    {
      address = "192.168.1.2";   # the static address you want
      prefixLength = 24;        # netmask /24 (255.255.255.0)
    }
  ];

  # (optional) set the default gateway and DNS servers
  networking.defaultGateway = "192.168.1.1";
  networking.nameservers = [ "8.8.8.8" ];
}
```

This snippet follows the example given in the NixOS manual for IPv4 static configuration [2]. After adding it, rebuild the system (`sudo nixos-rebuild switch`) and the interface `eth0` will use the specified static IPv4 address.

To disable the NixOS firewall, set its enable option to `false` in your configuration:

```nix
{
  networking.firewall.enable = false;
}
```

After adding this to your `configuration.nix`, rebuild the system (e.g., `nixos-rebuild switch`).  

[1]

To open a range of TCP ports you use the firewall option **`networking.firewall.allowedTCPPortRanges`** and specify the start (`from`) and end (`to`) of each range:

```nix
{
  networking.firewall.allowedTCPPortRanges = [
    {
      from = 4000;
      to   = 4007;
    }
  ];
}
```

This will open ports 4000 through 4007 for incoming TCP traffic. [1]

Yes. NixOS can keep itself up‑to‑date automatically by enabling the built‑in auto‑upgrade service. Add the following options to **/etc/nixos/configuration.nix**:

```nix
{
  system.autoUpgrade.enable = true;      # turn on the periodic upgrade service
  system.autoUpgrade.allowReboot = true; # (optional) reboot automatically when a new kernel or initrd is installed
  # You can also pin a specific channel, e.g.
  # system.autoUpgrade.channel = "https://channels.nixos.org/nixos-26.05";
}
```

This creates the `nixos-upgrade.service` timer, which periodically runs `nixos-rebuild switch --upgrade` (or reboots automatically if `allowReboot` is true) to upgrade the system to the latest version in the configured channel [1].

Add a garbage‑collection timer in your `configuration.nix` by enabling the automatic GC unit and specifying the time you want it to run, e.g.:

```nix
{
  nix.gc.automatic = true;
  nix.gc.dates = "03:15";
}
```

When you rebuild the system, NixOS will create the `nix-gc.service` timer that runs `nix-collect-garbage` each night at the given time. [1]

To enable the GNOME graphical desktop on NixOS, add the following to your `configuration.nix`:

```nix
{
  services.desktopManager.gnome.enable = true;
  services.displayManager.gdm.enable = true;
}
```

This activates the GNOME desktop environment and the GDM display manager (recommended for full GNOME functionality)【1】.

To enable the OpenSSH server, add the following setting to your NixOS configuration:

```nix
{
  services.openssh.enable = true;
}
```

This activates the SSH daemon on the system. [1]

To disable root logins over SSH you set the OpenSSH option `PermitRootLogin` to `"no"` in your NixOS configuration:

```nix
{
  services.openssh.enable = true;
  services.openssh.settings.PermitRootLogin = "no";
}
```

This disables all root logins, not just password‑based ones. [1]

You can declare a user’s authorized SSH public keys in the NixOS configuration like this:

```nix
{
  # Enable the SSH service
  services.openssh.enable = true;

  # Declare the keys for a specific user (e.g., alice)
  users.users.alice.openssh.authorizedKeys.keys = [
    "ssh-ed25519 AAAAB3NzaC1kc3MAAACBAPIkGWVEt4..."
  ];
}
```

The `users.users.<name>.openssh.authorizedKeys.keys` attribute takes a list of public‑key strings, which will be installed as the user’s `~/.ssh/authorized_keys` file. [1]

To assign a static IPv4 address to **eth0**, add a static address definition (and optionally a gateway and DNS servers) to your NixOS configuration:

```nix
{
  networking.interfaces.eth0.ipv4.addresses = [
    {
      address = "192.168.1.2";
      prefixLength = 24;
    }
  ];

  networking.defaultGateway = "192.168.1.1";
  networking.nameservers = [ "8.8.8.8" ];
}
```

This disables DHCP for *eth0* and configures the interface with the specified address, netmask (via `prefixLength`), default gateway, and DNS servers. After editing `configuration.nix`, rebuild the system (`sudo nixos-rebuild switch`).  

