To allow HTTP (port 80) and HTTPS (port 443) through the NixOS firewall, add the ports to `networking.firewall.allowedTCPPorts` in your configuration (the firewall is enabled by default, so you only need to open the ports you want):

```nix
{
  # The firewall is enabled by default; no need to set it to true.
  networking.firewall.allowedTCPPorts = [
    80   # HTTP
    443  # HTTPS
  ];
}
```

This will open the two TCP ports to the outside world while keeping the rest of the firewall rules in place. [1]

To disable the NixOS firewall, set the `networking.firewall.enable` option to `false` in your configuration:

```nix
{
  networking.firewall.enable = false;
}
```

This turns off the stateful firewall that is enabled by default. [1]

To open a contiguous range of TCP ports you use the **`networking.firewall.allowedTCPPortRanges`** option and specify the start (`from`) and end (`to`) of the range:

```nix
{
  networking.firewall.allowedTCPPortRanges = [
    {
      from = 4000;
      to   = 4007;
    }
  ];
}
```

This adds ports 4000 through 4007 to the firewall’s allowed list. [1]

Create the user declaratively in **configuration.nix** and add it to the **wheel** group (the group that grants sudo rights).

```nix
# /etc/nixos/configuration.nix
{
  users.users.alice = {
    isNormalUser = true;          # ordinary (non‑system) account
    description   = "Alice";
    extraGroups   = [ "wheel" "networkmanager" ];   # wheel → sudo, networkmanager → network control
    # optional: give the user a password (otherwise login requires a key)
    # hashedPassword = "<result of mkpasswd …>";
  };
}
```

* The `extraGroups = [ "wheel" … ]` line makes the user a member of the **wheel** group, which “allows her to use sudo to execute commands as root” [1].
* Users created this way have no password by default; you can set one later with `passwd` or by providing a `hashedPassword` [1].

After adding the snippet, apply it with:

```sh
sudo nixos-rebuild switch
```

The user **alice** will now exist, belong to the wheel group, and be able to run commands with `sudo`.

Yes. NixOS can upgrade itself automatically by enabling the built‑in auto‑upgrade service. Add the following to your `configuration.nix`:

```nix
{
  system.autoUpgrade.enable = true;
  system.autoUpgrade.allowReboot = true;   # optional – reboots when a new kernel is installed
  # optional – specify a channel explicitly
  # system.autoUpgrade.channel = "https://channels.nixos.org/nixos-26.05";
}
```

This creates the `nixos-upgrade.service` timer, which periodically runs `nixos-rebuild switch --upgrade` (or reboots automatically if `allowReboot` is true) to keep the system up‑to‑date [1].

To enable the GNOME graphical desktop on NixOS, add the following settings to your `configuration.nix`:

```nix
{
  services.desktopManager.gnome.enable = true;
  services.displayManager.gdm.enable = true;
}
```

This activates the full GNOME desktop environment and the GDM display manager (recommended for full GNOME functionality such as screen locking)【1】. After updating the configuration, run `sudo nixos-rebuild switch` (or `nixos-rebuild boot`) to apply the changes.
